package com.veilbreaker.companion

/** Offline engineering context; examples are illustrative, never captured evidence. */
internal object CellRfReferenceDetails {
    fun sections(id: String): List<Pair<String, String>> = when (id) {
        "cell-power", "expanded-cell-rsrp" -> listOf(
            "Read the measurement" to "LTE RSRP measures LTE reference-signal power. NR SS-RSRP measures synchronization-signal power and must be labeled separately. Android documents LTE RSRP from −140 to −43 dBm; this API range is not a service target. NR SS-RSRP has its own reported range. An unavailable value is not zero or a weak signal.",
            "Field comparison" to "Compare the same SIM, radio technology, band and cell at known times and receiver orientations. Example: an LTE reading changing from −110 to −95 dBm is a 15 dB increase, but does not by itself establish better SINR, throughput or a different tower. Repeat after allowing the modem to settle and record sample age. Sources: https://developer.android.com/reference/android/telephony/CellSignalStrengthLte and https://developer.android.com/reference/android/telephony/CellSignalStrengthNr")
        "cell-quality" -> listOf(
            "Interpretation" to "RSRQ and SINR describe different radio properties. A strong RSRP alongside poor reported quality is a reason to investigate, not proof of a particular interferer. Keep LTE RSSNR and NR SS-SINR associated with their own technology and sample time; Android may omit either.",
            "Use with service results" to "Record RSRP, RSRQ and SINR alongside a repeatable throughput and latency test on the intended cellular interface. Compare like-for-like locations, time windows and cell identities. Network load, mobility, scheduling and backhaul can affect service independently of the reported signal. Sources: https://developer.android.com/reference/android/telephony/CellSignalStrengthLte and https://developer.android.com/reference/android/telephony/CellSignalStrengthNr")
        "cell-identity", "expanded-cell-pci" -> listOf(
            "Identity hierarchy" to "PCI is a local physical-cell identifier that can be reused; it is not a globally unique tower ID. Record the reported technology, MCC/MNC, cell identifier, TAC, channel, PCI and time together when exposed. Each field may be absent, cached or scoped to a particular subscription.",
            "Field use and limits" to "When a value changes, check which identity fields changed and whether the receiver moved or switched technology. A nearby reference-database candidate is a lead, not proof of a serving antenna. Shared DAS systems can distribute a cell identity across multiple remotes. Source: https://developer.android.com/reference/android/telephony/CellIdentityLte")
        "expanded-cell-nr" -> listOf(
            "Power and quality" to "SS-RSRP, SS-RSRQ and SS-SINR refer to synchronization-signal measurements; CSI measurements are different fields and should not be relabeled as SS results. Android documents separate ranges and can return unavailable when a modem or permission does not expose a field.",
            "Compare deliberately" to "Record the reported NR cell, band, subscription, age and receiver, then compare another NR observation at a known position. A single strong SS-RSRP is not a capacity test, and a missing NR metric does not establish that the environment has no 5G signal. Source: https://developer.android.com/reference/android/telephony/CellSignalStrengthNr")
        "expanded-cell-anchor" -> listOf(
            "NSA and SA" to "A non-standalone deployment can use an LTE anchor together with NR, while standalone NR uses NR for the primary cellular connection. Android display indicators and data-network type are not a complete modem protocol trace; carrier aggregation and secondary cells may be hidden.",
            "Investigation" to "Capture the selected subscription's registered LTE and NR observations separately, with each cell's age and role if exposed. Record changes during a repeatable transfer and compare service results; do not infer that a displayed 5G icon establishes every band in use. Source: https://developer.android.com/reference/android/telephony/TelephonyDisplayInfo")
        "expanded-cell-band" -> listOf(
            "Band, channel and direction" to "A band defines an operating range and duplex arrangement; a channel number identifies a raster position within a radio technology. Uplink and downlink can use separate frequency ranges or share spectrum by time, depending on the band. Do not assume the same channel number means the same frequency across LTE and NR.",
            "Use a band plan" to "Record the modem-reported band and EARFCN or NR-ARFCN where available, then check the applicable 3GPP band table and current allocation. A band listed for a carrier is a general association, not proof of the operator or physical transmitter at this location. Source: https://portal.3gpp.org/Specifications.aspx")
        "expanded-cell-das" -> listOf(
            "Indoor interpretation" to "A DAS distributes RF through components and remote antennas. Several remotes can share a cell identifier, and walls or reflections can change a handset reading. The phone reports its serving or neighboring radio data; it does not locate the physical remote providing that energy.",
            "Repeatable survey" to "Use one floor coordinate system at a time, capture at repeatable heights and orientations, and mark operator placements separately from GPS. Record measured power and quality, relevant obstruction notes and a consistent service test; retest anomalies without attributing a cell to a specific remote without independent evidence. Source: https://developer.android.com/reference/android/telephony/CellInfo")
        "expanded-cell-sim" -> listOf(
            "Subscription scope" to "On a multi-SIM phone, service state and modem observations are scoped to a subscription where Android exposes them. The default data subscription and the selected measurement subscription need not be the same; an active SIM does not itself establish the path used by an application test.",
            "Verification" to "Select the intended subscription before collecting. Record its reported carrier and the interface selected for the network test. If subscription selection, permission or registration changes, repeat the capture and preserve the earlier evidence. Source: https://developer.android.com/reference/android/telephony/SubscriptionManager")
        "expanded-cell-age" -> listOf(
            "Receipt and sample age" to "A cell callback can contain cached observations. The receipt time says when the application received a response; reported cell age describes the age of that modem observation at receipt. GPS can be acquired separately, so a single capture timestamp does not prove both were simultaneous.",
            "Avoid false trends" to "For a trend, look for distinct reported modem timestamps and a consistent subscription and cell identity. Repeated cached values are not independent samples. Android notes that cell updates can be rate-limited and are not guaranteed. Source: https://developer.android.com/reference/android/telephony/TelephonyManager#requestCellInfoUpdate(java.util.concurrent.Executor,%20android.telephony.TelephonyManager.CellInfoCallback)")
        "rf-power", "expanded-dbm-table", "expanded-db-ratio-table" -> listOf(
            "Units and reference" to "dB is a ratio; dBm is power relative to 1 mW; dBi is antenna gain relative to an isotropic radiator. Convert power using mW = 10^(dBm/10). For equal impedances voltage ratios use 20 log10, while power ratios use 10 log10.",
            "Examples" to "0 dBm = 1 mW, 10 dBm = 10 mW and 30 dBm = 1 W. Adding approximately 3.01 dB doubles power; subtracting 10 dB divides it by ten. These are calculated conversions, not observed measurements. Do not add an antenna's dBi to a receiver reading as though it were cable power. Source: https://www.itu.int/rec/R-REC-P.525-5-202411-I/en")
        "rf-noise", "expanded-noise-figure" -> listOf(
            "Thermal baseline" to "At 290 K, the ideal matched thermal-noise density is approximately −174 dBm/Hz. For equivalent noise bandwidth B in Hz, estimate input noise as −174 + 10 log10(B) dBm; add receiver noise figure in dB for a simplified input-referred receiver estimate.",
            "Worked example and limits" to "At 20 MHz the ideal thermal result is approximately −101 dBm; with a 5 dB noise figure the simplified estimate is about −96 dBm. This is a calculation, not an observed SDR floor. External noise, bandwidth definition, gain and calibration affect measurements. Source: https://go.keysight.com/us/en/assets/7018-06808/application-notes/5952-8255.pdf")
        "rf-obstacles", "expanded-multipath" -> listOf(
            "Propagation mechanisms" to "Materials can absorb, reflect, transmit or scatter energy; edges can diffract it. Paths arriving with different phases may reinforce or cancel each other, so a small receiver movement can change both power and quality. A phone measurement cannot identify the object responsible.",
            "Operator workflow" to "Mark the obstacle material, dimensions if known, floor, antenna orientation and position as operator observations. Capture repeat readings at multiple nearby locations and at another time, changing one variable at a time. Material loss depends on frequency, construction, geometry and moisture. Source: https://www.itu.int/rec/R-REC-P.2040/en")
        "expanded-fspl" -> listOf(
            "Equation and example" to "For distance d in km and frequency f in MHz, ideal free-space loss is approximately 32.4 + 20 log10(d) + 20 log10(f) dB. At 1 km and 2400 MHz it is about 100.0 dB. Doubling distance adds approximately 6 dB; antenna gains are not included in this loss term.",
            "Interpretation" to "Enter this ideal loss once in a link budget, with antenna gains and cable losses accounted for separately. Terrain, walls, clutter, multipath and atmospheric conditions are absent; the calculation is not a coverage prediction or proof of service. Source: ITU-R P.525-5, https://www.itu.int/rec/R-REC-P.525-5-202411-I/en")
        "expanded-fresnel" -> listOf(
            "Zone geometry" to "For a path with distances d1 and d2 from an obstacle and wavelength λ in matching units, the first-zone radius is sqrt(λ d1 d2 / (d1 + d2)). It is widest near the midpoint. Clearance is measured from the direct antenna-to-antenna path, not from the ground.",
            "Use and limits" to "Document antenna phase-center heights, total path, frequency and obstacle height; identify trees and buildings missing from terrain data. A 60% first-zone clearance is a common planning heuristic, not a guarantee. Curvature, refraction and non-ideal obstacles affect the actual result. Source: https://www.itu.int/rec/R-REC-P.526/en")
        "expanded-earth" -> listOf(
            "Effective Earth model" to "A terrain profile can represent atmospheric refraction by using an effective Earth radius k times the physical radius. The commonly used k = 4/3 is an assumption for standard conditions, not measured refraction at the site. The path bulge is largest near its midpoint.",
            "Practical checks" to "Confirm terrain elevation datum and sample coverage, both antenna heights above ground, path distance and the selected k value. Include building and vegetation heights separately where available. Recheck long or marginal paths under the local atmospheric conditions. Source: https://www.itu.int/rec/R-REC-P.526/en")
        "expanded-wavelength" -> listOf(
            "Free-space conversion" to "Wavelength λ = c/f, with c approximately 299,792,458 m/s and frequency f in Hz. Conveniently, λ in meters is about 0.3 divided by frequency in GHz. Thus 2.4 GHz is approximately 0.125 m, or 12.5 cm.",
            "Cable and antennas" to "In cable, propagation velocity is the velocity factor times c; electrical wavelength and delay therefore differ from free space. A quarter-wave physical element can also differ from λ/4 due to geometry and loading. Verify the actual device or antenna design. Source: https://www.itu.int/rec/R-REC-P.525-5-202411-I/en")
        "expanded-eirp" -> listOf(
            "Signal chain" to "For a specified transmit power at a known reference plane, EIRP in dBm = transmitter power in dBm − feedline/component loss in dB + antenna gain in dBi. Example: 20 dBm − 3 dB + 10 dBi = 27 dBm EIRP in the antenna's gain direction.",
            "Regulatory context" to "The example is calculated, not measured; confirm channel, bandwidth, antenna pattern and the applicable conducted-power and EIRP rules for the jurisdiction and radio. Never assume a carrier-band association establishes authorization. Source: https://www.itu.int/rec/R-REC-P.525-5-202411-I/en")
        "expanded-coax" -> listOf(
            "Loss budget" to "Use the cable manufacturer's attenuation at the operating frequency and actual run length. If a cable is rated 6 dB per 100 ft at the frequency of interest, 50 ft contributes approximately 3 dB before connectors and other components. The same passive cable attenuates in either direction.",
            "Field checks" to "Record cable type, length, frequency, connector count and measurement plane. Water, damage, wrong adapters or mismatch can add loss beyond a nominal table; inspect and measure when possible. Do not treat an estimate as a calibrated cable test. Source: https://www.itu.int/rec/R-REC-P.525/en")
        "expanded-reflection" -> listOf(
            "Relationships" to "For reflection-coefficient magnitude |Γ|, VSWR = (1+|Γ|)/(1−|Γ|), return loss = −20 log10(|Γ|), and reflected-power fraction = |Γ|². An ideal match has zero reflection and VSWR 1:1; its return loss tends to infinity.",
            "Measurement plane" to "Measure with a calibrated instrument and identify the reference plane. Cable attenuation between instrument and antenna can make the measured return loss look better than the antenna's actual match. A good match alone does not establish gain, direction or radiated efficiency. Source: https://www.keysight.com/used/rs/en/knowledge/guides/spectrum-analyzer-buying-guide/how-to-measure-return-loss-with-a-spectrum-analyzer")
        "expanded-pol" -> listOf(
            "Ideal linear case" to "For ideal linearly polarized antennas at relative angle θ, the power coupling factor is cos²(θ); the corresponding loss is −10 log10(cos² θ). At 45° the ideal loss is approximately 3 dB. Perfect ideal orthogonality tends toward zero coupling, but real isolation is finite.",
            "Field use" to "Check transmit and receive polarization, physical rotation and connector-to-element mapping before moving an antenna. Reflections can alter the received field, so the ideal calculation is not a measurement or a complete multipath model. Source: https://scdn.rohde-schwarz.com/ur/pws/dl_downloads/premiumdownloads/premium_dl_brochures_and_datasheets/premium_dl_whitepaper/Antenna_Basics_8GE01_1e.pdf")
        "expanded-gain" -> listOf(
            "Pattern versus peak" to "Antenna gain is direction-dependent. The published peak gain in dBi applies in the direction specified by the pattern; horizontal and vertical beamwidth, sidelobes, electrical tilt and polarization matter when the receiver is off-axis.",
            "Alignment workflow" to "Record mounting height, azimuth, mechanical tilt, polarization and the manufacturer's pattern. Compare the intended bearing and elevation against that pattern before assuming a peak gain in the link budget. A nearby structure or installation can change the realized pattern. Source: https://scdn.rohde-schwarz.com/ur/pws/dl_downloads/premiumdownloads/premium_dl_brochures_and_datasheets/premium_dl_whitepaper/Antenna_Basics_8GE01_1e.pdf")
        "expanded-fade" -> listOf(
            "Calculated margin" to "A simplified fade margin is predicted received power minus the receiver threshold for the selected mode. Example: −70 dBm predicted receive level against −90 dBm sensitivity gives 20 dB margin, provided both numbers refer to compatible conditions and reference planes.",
            "Validate both directions" to "Use the sensitivity for the required bandwidth, modulation and error criterion. Include feedline and component losses and evaluate uplink separately. Margin alone is not a service-availability guarantee; multipath, rain, interference and equipment behavior require appropriate modeling and field verification. Source: https://www.itu.int/rec/R-REC-P.525-5-202411-I/en")
        else -> emptyList()
    }
}
